course: dictionary
lesson: 13444
title: Hospitalise and the story behind it
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Hospitalise is a verb. It means to send someone to hospital, or to keep them there for treatment, because they are ill or hurt. «Three people were hospitalised after the crash.»
explain: Look at the clues. A crash is an accident, so the people were hurt and needed hospital care. The word is often used with was or were, as in «was hospitalised», because something happens to the person.
explain: Test your meaning in the sentence: Three people were sent to hospital after the crash. It still makes sense, so you have it right. Safety lessons use this word to show why we follow rules about roads, water and fire.
fact: Hospitalise is a verb. Its forms are hospitalise, hospitalises, hospitalised and hospitalising.
fact: «Hospitalised» means sent to hospital, or kept there for treatment, because of illness or injury.
fact: Hospitalise is often used in the passive, as in «He was hospitalised with a broken leg».
fact: «Hospitalised for a week» means kept in hospital for one week.
fact: In American English the spelling is hospitalize, with a z.
exercise: «Three people were hospitalised after the crash.» What does hospitalised mean? | *Sent to hospital for treatment | Told to go home and rest | Tested for a driving licence | Given a new car
exercise: In «He was hospitalised with a broken arm», what caused him to go to hospital? | A holiday | *A broken arm | A school trip | A new bicycle
exercise: Why do safety lessons talk about people being hospitalised? | To explain how to become a doctor | To show that hospitals are very big buildings | *To show how rules about roads, water and fire can prevent serious injuries | To show that crashes are always funny
test: «Dan was hospitalised with flu.» Which word means nearly the same as hospitalised? | Given a prize | Sent to school | *Sent to hospital | Sent home for a holiday
test: Which is the American English spelling of hospitalise? | *hospitalize | hospitalsed | hospitalice | hospitalis
exam: Which sentence uses hospitalised correctly? | «The cake was hospitalised at lunch time.» | «I hospitalised my bag to school.» | *«The driver was hospitalised after the accident.» | «The sun hospitalised the sky.»
